=== Introduction
--- Psalms 41–43 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 41–43
sub: He that considereth the poor • As the hart panteth • Hope thou in God
refs: Psalm 41:1; Psalm 42:1; Psalm 42:11
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 41
--- Psalm 41 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 41
sub: Blessed is he that considereth the poor
refs: Psalm 41:1

--- He that considereth the poor
kicker: Psalm 41 · Blessed
big: Psalm 41:1 | mark: Blessed is he that considereth the poor
p: **Psalm 41** is brief and to the point. David opens with the doctrine of the Lord's promise to preserve and give long life on the earth to those of His people who consider the poor. Who show mercy to the widows and take up the cause of the fatherless.
p: Just as the commandment to honor your parents comes with a promise, that it be well with you and lengthen your days, this practice of pure religion, to consider the poor, comes with a similar blessing.
p: Basic stuff, which we are told over and over again in scripture, because it often is the hardest thing for man to do; love the unlovable the way God loves us.

--- They cannot recompense thee
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:1
scripture: Luke 14:13-14 | mark: for they cannot recompense thee
p: The act of giving alms or feeding the poor is not itself what gives us blessings. Many may be generous in their actions, to be seen of men or to make themselves self-righteous.

--- Love unfeigned
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:1
p: Helping people who cannot help you back out of love, is love unfeigned. It is the charity Paul calls us to. The selfless, sacrificial love for God and others, in your heart. More so than the actual alms or donation of goods. The spirit behind it is everything.
big: 1 Corinthians 13:3 | mark: and have not charity, it profiteth me nothing

--- Upon the bed of languishing
kicker: Psalm 41 · The Lord will preserve him
scripture: Psalm 41:2-3 | mark: The LORD will strengthen him upon the bed of languishing
p: So we see that the love behind the care given to the poor, is rewarded with strengthening us in our sickness, delivering us from our enemies, God showing to us the mercy we showed those who could never repay us for the kindness.
p: Those that do it for their own reasons, have their reward. But the heart behind it is what God wants from us.

--- Unto one of the least of these
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:1–3
big: Matthew 25:40 | mark: ye have done it unto me
big: Proverbs 19:17 | mark: lendeth unto the LORD

--- A bountiful eye
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:1–3
p: God loves a cheerful giver, one who isn't seeking anything for himself.
big: Proverbs 22:9
p: But those that oppress the poor, God hates.
big: Proverbs 14:31 | mark: He that oppresseth the poor reproacheth his Maker

--- Reminders and confession
kicker: Psalm 41 · A pattern of prayer
p: David establishes this truth early in the psalm for a reason, which is made clear as it goes along.
p: It isn't so much as David brags about his charitable heart to the poor, but in the opening verses, he humbly reminds himself and the Lord he is petitioning of the promises He has made.
p: Then, the prayer moves to confession. The honest assessment of the heart before a holy, holy, holy God.
p: This pattern of reminders and confession, before petition, is helpful to keep in mind for our prayer life, if we want it to be successful.

--- Prayer warriors
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: We often hear about "prayer warriors," those who are continuously in prayer, especially for others. Who seek the burdens of others to bring them before the Lord, and it is a wonderful work.
p: But as we seek to be counted among the elite forces of prayer, we should always keep in mind the humility and confession that makes our prayer life effective.

--- Heal my soul
kicker: Psalm 41 · For I have sinned
big: Psalm 41:4 | mark: heal my soul; for I have sinned against thee
p: And so when David writes *be merciful unto me: heal my soul; for I have sinned against thee*, we need to take it to heart in our own approach to the Throne. The man who was the apple of God's eye was honest in his confession. The King of Israel was lowly enough to be convicted as he approached the Throne of Grace.

--- Begin with confession
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: Watch out for the creeping pride that may come from being recognized as a "prayer warrior," and the audacity that may infect what is a great habit. The loss of the love of others that should drive it, but iniquity creeping in.
p: Begin with confession. A contrite heart with love unfeigned can move mountains.

--- When shall he die?
kicker: Psalm 41 · Mine enemies
scripture: Psalm 41:5-6 | mark: When shall he die, and his name perish?
p: The remainder of **Psalm 41** gives context, a sickly sorrow from illness has come. And in his weakness, David sees the opportunists and haters using his failed health to speak ill against him.
p: They are backbiters, praising him with vanities and flattery to his face, but quietly questioning "when will he die?" and when can they take over. Cursing him by claiming God was punishing him.

--- An evil disease, say they
kicker: Psalm 41 · Whisper together
scripture: Psalm 41:7-8 | mark: All that hate me whisper together against me
p: He faces betrayal and evil, even as the misery of illness besets him.

--- Mine own familiar friend
kicker: Psalm 41 · Betrayal
big: Psalm 41:9 | mark: hath lifted up his heel against me
p: During his low point of illness and enemies scheming, David mentions betrayal.
p: The truth is that we often face betrayal at the most inopportune times, when we are at our lowest. Or even those we trusted and rely on, letting us down when we need them the most.
p: This truth is one of the most devastating things we grapple with in life. Not that there are bad people who seek our harm, but there are close friends, even brothers, who turn on us in our darkest hours.

--- Thou favourest me
kicker: Psalm 41 · Raise me up
scripture: Psalm 41:10-12 | mark: be merciful unto me, and raise me up; settest me before thy face for ever
p: So, David has reminded God of His healing promises for considering the poor, he has confessed his sins before God. He has laid out the problem he faces, and then asks for God's help to deliver him.
p: He pleads for the Lord to be merciful. To remember His promises and forgiveness. And asks for this petition to be answered that David may know he is favored by the grace shown to him. That the evil men do not triumph, and give them cause to scoff those that trust in the name of the Lord.
p: Finally, he closes this prayerful lament with the truth that God has upheld him, and saved him, forever. His salvation remains regardless of health or men's evil or anything else that God wills. And with that blessed hope, he closes in praise for the Almighty.

--- Amen, and Amen
kicker: Psalm 41 · Blessed be the Lord
big: Psalm 41:13

--- He that eateth bread with me
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:9
p: *But thou, O LORD, be merciful unto me, and raise me up, that I may requite them.*
p: Jesus explicitly quotes **Psalm 41:9** during the Last Supper in **John 13:18**;
big: John 13:18 | mark: He that eateth bread with me hath lifted up his heel against me
p: David’s specific pain was a literal prophecy of Christ’s betrayal by Judas. Jesus knows our infirmities, He has suffered our pains. He knows what it is like to be betrayed by those closest to Him. (Not only the evil of Judas, but also being denied by Peter, who loves Him)

--- A friend that sticketh closer than a brother
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:9
p: But, thanks be to God that He is steadfast and true. The Lord raises us up, and gives us the strength to overcome their evil, that it all turn out good, because He loves us.
big: Proverbs 18:24 | mark: there is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother
p: That friend is Jesus Christ.

--- They of his own household
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:9
p: Even our family may turn against us.
scripture: Matthew 10:34-36 | mark: a man’s foes shall be they of his own household

--- I will never leave thee
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 41:9
p: But we have the assurance and knowledge that He will always be there for us. *For he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee*.
big: Deuteronomy 31:6 | mark: he will not fail thee, nor forsake thee
p: We can take comfort in Jesus' own promise to us:…*and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.*
center: Amen.

=== Psalm 42
--- Psalm 42 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 42
sub: As the hart panteth after the water brooks
refs: Psalm 42:1

--- As the hart panteth
kicker: Psalm 42 · Thirst
scripture: Psalm 42:1-2 | mark: As the hart panteth after the water brooks; My soul thirsteth for God, for the living God
p: **Psalm 42** is a gut-punch of a song, a prayer of desperation from a troubled believer, being strengthened in their faith, while simultaneously going through the wringer.
p: The psalmists (this psalm is attributed to the "Sons of Korah," likely the musicians and singers in the Temple) write of a man whose soul is panting after the Lord, as a hart, or deer, pants after the water of a brook. The thirst of need.

--- My tears have been my meat
kicker: Psalm 42 · Where is thy God?
big: Psalm 42:3 | mark: My tears have been my meat day and night
p: A plaintive lament, needing the Lord so badly that we'd like to die, just to be with Him and at peace. *Tears have been my meat,* the psalmist says. All he has consumed, day and night, while scoffers say, *Where is thy God?*
p: The spiritual thirst, though tears have fed him, is a powerful metaphor. But we have the promise of living waters, in which we shall never thirst. The promise that our spirit will be quenched in Christ.

--- A well of water
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 42:2
big: John 4:14 | mark: shall never thirst
p: This promise is true and we can count on it, but the trials of this life can leave us panting for the living water, to be satisfied by them in the thirst we develop during low points in our lives.

--- When I remember these things
kicker: Psalm 42 · Why art thou cast down?
scripture: Psalm 42:4-5 | mark: When I remember these things; Why art thou cast down, O my soul?
p: The psalmist writes of the remembrance of his joy in the Lord, the times he sang and worshipped in the congregation during holidays, and asks himself why is he so down and depressed. He knows he will yet praise God, when He turns His face towards him.
p: Knowing that in the past, he's been delivered and trusting it will happen again, isn't just a comfort but in a way, a condemnation against himself.

--- Unshakable faith
kicker: Application • Cast down
p: "Why am I so down hearted in my soul, when I can remember and appreciate all God has done for me, and my faith that he will again?" This is a place that even a mature Christian can come to. It is the truth that the weariness of this life can beat us down, even with unshakable faith.
p: As Pastor Jack Hyles often preached, you can't have unshakable faith unless your faith has been shaken.
p: The troubles become the source for the strength of our conviction. Because in trouble we are shown His power.

--- As with a sword in my bones
kicker: Psalm 42 · Mine enemies reproach me
big: Psalm 42:10 | mark: As with a sword in my bones, mine enemies reproach me
p: And in our darkest moments, the voices of the scoffers cut like a knife. Satan uses them to attack our faith, as he sought to shake Job's.

--- Doth Job fear God for nought?
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 42:10
scripture: Job 1:8-11 | mark: Doth Job fear God for nought?
p: The mocking of the unrighteous, who delight to see God's children stumble, to say "Aha" when we fall, stems from this same desire Satan has, when he accused the brethren day and night before the Lord. Seeking whom he may devour.

--- We glory in tribulations
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 42:5
p: But we have the peace of God in tribulation, even if we are despondent in the moment, we know He is true.
scripture: Romans 5:1-6 | mark: tribulation worketh patience; hope maketh not ashamed
p: We were delivered from death into new life, at our worst. The hope we have in Christ will never leave us ashamed. Let them laugh us to scorn, we shall be saved and the Lord will cast them down.

--- I will remember thee
kicker: Psalm 42 · My soul is cast down
big: Psalm 42:6 | mark: therefore will I remember thee
p: In our lowest, let us remember Him. Let our faith be spread across the land. Let us cry out to those that doubt, that our God is our rock and our refuge.
p: But the light of the Lord can still seem dim and distant when our heart is troubled. We can acknowledge our own pain and suffering, and cry out to the Lord during it, that He may use it to purify ourselves.
big: 1 Peter 4:13 | mark: partakers of Christ’s sufferings
p: Suffering leads to glory.

--- Deep calleth unto deep
kicker: Psalm 42 · His song shall be with me
scripture: Psalm 42:7-8 | mark: Deep calleth unto deep; in the night his song shall be with me
p: *Deep calleth unto deep,* our soul, overwhelmed by sorrow, drowning in sadness, calls out to the Lord.
p: This hope is the anchor to our soul. Even when we feel He has forgotten us.

--- Why hast thou forgotten me?
kicker: Psalm 42 · God my rock
big: Psalm 42:9 | mark: I will say unto God my rock, Why hast thou forgotten me?
p: The prayer acknowledging God is our rock, even as we ask "Why?" Why is it that we continue in sorrow and are oppressed by our enemies? Those who afflict us in our grief, and cast doubt on our faith and the power of our God, while we wallow in tears. We may seek the Lord, and ask Him "Why?"
p: But in the end, while we may be down on ourselves for letting our depression and sadness get us down, we can trust in the faithfulness of God.

--- Hope thou in God
kicker: Psalm 42 · The health of my countenance
big: Psalm 42:11 | mark: hope thou in God
center: Amen.

--- Faith isn't the absence of trouble
kicker: Application • Cast down
p: *Why art thou cast down, O my soul?* It is a comfort to any believer to realize that remembering past deliverances doesn't automatically cure present depression.
p: We may take the moment of our pain and beat ourselves up for not appreciating what God has done for us. For allowing sadness and heartache to linger. Or that sorrow to affect us in ways that makes everything worse, when our own memories of past deliverance aren't enough to make us snap out of it, and we question and blame ourselves.
p: True conviction is forged precisely when the storms are howling. Faith isn't the absence of trouble; it is the stubborn decision to anchor oneself to the Rock when you feel like you are drowning.

--- This too shall pass
kicker: Application • Cast down
p: Every life has seasons of strife. We shouldn't wallow in self-pity, but we can recognize our need for working through grief without beating ourselves up for it.
p: We know the truth of Christ. We will sing His praise again. This too shall pass. But in the moment, it's okay to *say unto God my rock, Why hast thou forgotten me?* Just don't take this feeling as an excuse to forget God.

=== Psalm 43
--- Psalm 43 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 43
sub: O send out thy light and thy truth
refs: Psalm 43:1

--- Plead my cause
kicker: Psalm 43 · Judge me, O God
scripture: Psalm 43:1-2 | mark: Judge me, O God, and plead my cause; For thou art the God of my strength
p: **Psalm 43** is a shorter song of the same theme as **Psalm 42,** a condensed appeal to the Lord in desperate times.
p: The call to judge, that is to be a deliverer, an advocate, to bring the cause of the prayerful against the ungodly that afflict them, is the heart of it.

--- A spiritual siege
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 43:1
p: We may not be in the straits of captivity or war, or even persecution by our own government for the cause of Christ, and praise God for sparing us, but modern American Christians can find ourselves in calamity and a spiritual siege.
big: Ephesians 6:12 | mark: we wrestle not against flesh and blood
p: We may face opposition by the ungodly in our families, workplace, neighborhoods, even in some churches, anywhere that we find our faith and longsuffering tested by the slings and arrows of those who hate us and our Lord. Jesus warned us.
big: John 15:18

--- Division
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 43:1
p: He warned us that He would bring division, even among family.
scripture: Luke 12:51-53 | mark: but rather division

--- The God of my strength
kicker: Application • A spiritual siege
p: In any of this, the lack of scale and immediate physical danger doesn't change the fact that our heart can grow despondent over our circumstances, and the spiritual battles can be as consequential as the real world wars, at times.
p: Reminding ourselves that Jesus is the God of our strength, the Rock of our salvation, even when we feel far from Him, is a common prayer in these Psalms, and with good reason.

--- Draw nigh to God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 43:2
p: In **Zechariah 1:3**, the Bible says *Turn ye unto me, saith the LORD of hosts, and I will turn unto you*. A promise of God, repeated in the Epistles;
big: Zechariah 1:3 | mark: Turn ye unto me, saith the LORD of hosts, and I will turn unto you
scripture: James 4:7-8 | mark: Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you

--- He will draw nigh
kicker: Application • Turn to God
p: The reminder to come to God in humble confession is key, but the overall concept that God seeks our repentance **towards** Him, that He will close the gap and draw nigh is a fundamental doctrine, and a hope we see promised and delivered time and again in scripture.
p: And in our peril, and in our desperate need, if we turn to God and humble ourselves before Him, and ask for His judgement, He will come to us and lift us up in His arms, and lead us to where we need to be.

--- Thy light and thy truth
kicker: Psalm 43 · Thy holy hill
big: Psalm 43:3 | mark: O send out thy light and thy truth: let them lead me
p: By His wonderful light and truth, we know the way to Him. Jesus said He is the way, the truth and the life, the light of the world, guiding us to His holy hill, the tents of His people, the mansions of glory.
p: By the gospel shared among the people, the desperate are led to the house of God. To the local church, where His praises are sung in our deliverance, and where mourning may turn to joy.

--- Then will I go
kicker: Psalm 43 · God my exceeding joy
big: Psalm 43:4 | mark: Then will I go unto the altar of God
p: The definitive *Then will I go*, shows the confidence in His promise. But the doubts of the flesh can cause us to be cast down. Depressed at the situation and wondering when God's hand will lift us up. The psalmist reconciles these two things in the person's spirit, this contradiction between the heart and the spirit, with the encouragement to hope in God.
p: To know *Then will I go* because He is faithful and true. That no matter what comes, we shall praise Him. Here in the congregation, or in the heavenly choir. He is our God. Amen

--- The shared refrain
kicker: Psalm 43 · Hope in God
big: Psalm 43:5 | mark: hope in God
p: **Psalms 42 and 43** share the refrain;
p: And there are no attributions given to **Psalm 43** in the Hebrew, the way **Psalm 42** is attributed to the "sons of Korah."
p: Some translations and traditions combine the two songs, to make it one continuous Psalm.

--- Two psalms
kicker: Psalm 43 · Kept separate
p: While no one can say with any certainty, I believe the KJV translators were wise and Spirit guided to keep them separated. While the themes are similar as far as the broken-hearted nature of them, and the repeated refrain, they each carry a weight and purpose unique to themselves.
p: The longer appeal in **Psalm 42** is more of a meditation on the despondency of man in these circumstances, while the more brief and succinct **Psalm 43** tips the balance more to the understanding of God's promises and the faith that overcomes the fear.
p: Yes we still find the lament of our diminished spirit, but the focus has shifted slightly to the certain hope.
p: Its brief nature makes it a more accessible devotional and gives someone who is coming out of depression a more focused resolution.

--- The poor of spirit
kicker: Application • The meek and lowly
p: But, taken together the last several Psalms show that the poor of spirit are exactly who God seeks to lift up. That His promises aren't only for the super-faithful warriors, but the meek and lowly.
p: It is good to have a God Who understands our infirmities, and His Word as a relatable resource in our time of need.

